<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<timedtext format="3">
<head>
<pen id="1" b="1" fc="#FFFFFF" fo="254" bc="#140804" bo="170" et="4" ec="#000000" fs="4" sz="110"/>
<pen id="2" b="1" i="1" fc="#FFD65A" fo="254" bc="#140804" bo="190" et="4" ec="#000000" fs="4" sz="115"/>
<wp id="1" ap="7" ah="50" av="92"/>
<ws id="1" ju="2"/>
</head>
<body>
<p t="1140" d="3919" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">Four thousand two hundred years ago, a people
from east of Mesopotamia stepped onto the</s></p>
<p t="5100" d="1180" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">stage of history:</s></p>
<p t="6480" d="939" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">the Gutians.</s></p>
<p t="7460" d="4240" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">They brought Akkad, the world's first
empire, to its knees and ruled Mesopotamia,</s></p>
<p t="11740" d="2620" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">the cradle of civilization,
for a full century.</s></p>
<p t="14440" d="2880" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">Sumerian scribes gave
them a name in fear: the</s></p>
<p t="17420" d="1559" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">Dragon of the Mountains.</s></p>
<p t="19080" d="1760" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">So who were these Gutians?</s></p>
<p t="20880" d="3960" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">How did they rule Mesopotamia, and
why do so many historians count</s></p>
<p t="24880" d="2620" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">them among the oldest
ancestors of the Kurds?</s></p>
<p t="27540" d="1540" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">Welcome to Dîrok.</s></p>
<p t="41200" d="3720" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">The land between the Tigris and the
Euphrates witnessed the greatest</s></p>
<p t="44960" d="1579" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">inventions in human history.</s></p>
<p t="46580" d="4239" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">The first cities, writing, the wheel
and written law were all born here.</s></p>
<p t="50860" d="4759" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">Sumerian cities such as Uruk, Ur,
Lagash and Nippur were the world's first</s></p>
<p t="55660" d="1059" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">centers of civilization.</s></p>
<p t="56800" d="4219" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">In the twenty-third century BC, Sargon
of Akkad conquered these cities one</s></p>
<p t="61060" d="3519" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">by one and founded the first
known empire in history.</s></p>
<p t="64620" d="3999" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">The Akkadian Empire stretched from
the Persian Gulf to the Mediterranean.</s></p>
<p t="68660" d="4000" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">Sargon's grandson Naram-Sin called
himself king of the four corners of the</s></p>
<p t="72700" d="1000" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">world.</s></p>
<p t="73980" d="3819" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">But the end of this great
empire would come from the east.</s></p>
<p t="83220" d="4339" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">Mesopotamian sources call the land
east of the Tigris, between the Lesser</s></p>
<p t="87600" d="2419" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">Zab and the Diyala rivers, Gutium.</s></p>
<p t="90060" d="4040" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">This was the homeland of the
Gutians, and the roads descending
to the Mesopotamian plain were</s></p>
<p t="94140" d="1019" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">under their control.</s></p>
<p t="95200" d="4159" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">Under Naram-Sin's son Shar-kali-sharri,
the struggle between Akkad and the</s></p>
<p t="99400" d="1260" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">Gutians intensified.</s></p>
<p t="100700" d="3479" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">On Akkadian tablets, years were named
after the important events that took</s></p>
<p t="104220" d="999" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">place in them.</s></p>
<p t="105260" d="2400" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">One of these year names reads:</s></p>
<p t="107700" d="3899" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="2">"The year Shar-kali-sharri
captured Sharlag, king of Gutium."</s></p>
<p t="111740" d="2339" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">But Akkad's victories did not last.</s></p>
<p t="114120" d="4040" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">The empire was shaken by revolts,
struggles for the throne and</s></p>
<p t="118200" d="1299" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">a great drought.</s></p>
<p t="119540" d="4219" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">After Shar-kali-sharri's death, Akkad
fell into such chaos that the Sumerian</s></p>
<p t="123800" d="3299" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">King List describes the period
with a mocking question.</s></p>
<p t="127140" d="2199" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="2">"Who was king, who was not king?"</s></p>
<p t="129380" d="4080" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="2">The famous Sumerian text "The Curse of
Agade" blames Akkad's fall on the wrath</s></p>
<p t="133500" d="4440" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">of the gods, and the people sent
to carry out that wrath are named.</s></p>
<p t="137980" d="680" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">The Gutians.</s></p>
<p t="138700" d="3400" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">The text describes them as a
people who could not be controlled.</s></p>
<p t="142140" d="4520" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">Around 2150 BC the Akkadian Empire
collapsed, and the royal cities</s></p>
<p t="146700" d="3419" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">of Mesopotamia passed into
the hands of the Gutians.</s></p>
<p t="155540" d="2900" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">The Sumerian King List
contains a plain record.</s></p>
<p t="158480" d="2320" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="2">"Kingship passed to the army of Gutium."</s></p>
<p t="161300" d="3340" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">The list describes the first period of
the Gutians with a curious sentence.</s></p>
<p t="164680" d="3300" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="2">"In the army of Gutium
there was at first no</s></p>
<p t="168020" d="1840" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">known king; they were their own kings."</s></p>
<p t="170080" d="4180" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">Many scholars read this sentence as
a sign that the Gutians were governed</s></p>
<p t="174300" d="3800" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">not by a single absolute ruler
but by a shared council or</s></p>
<p t="178140" d="1000" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">by tribal leaders.</s></p>
<p t="179180" d="4320" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">According to the list, twenty-one Gutian
kings reigned for about a hundred years.</s></p>
<p t="183540" d="2939" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">Most of their kings stayed on
the throne for only a few years.</s></p>
<p t="186520" d="2520" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">This suggests that
leadership did not pass from</s></p>
<p t="189080" d="2780" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">father to son but changed
hands among the tribes.</s></p>
<p t="192040" d="3680" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">The Gutians governed the Sumerian
cities through local governors.</s></p>
<p t="195760" d="4540" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">Lugal-annatum, governor of the city of
Umma, dated his own inscription "in the</s></p>
<p t="200340" d="1879" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">time of Si'um, king of Gutium."</s></p>
<p t="202260" d="4140" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">In other words, the cities kept
their own administrations but
recognized the sovereignty of the</s></p>
<p t="206440" d="539" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">Gutian king.</s></p>
<p t="207020" d="3460" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">The Gutians also took over Akkad's
language of government and its traditions.</s></p>
<p t="210520" d="4520" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">A mace head belonging to King La'erabum
reads, in Akkadian, "the mighty king</s></p>
<p t="215080" d="1140" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">of Gutium."</s></p>
<p t="216260" d="3080" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">The most remarkable of
these kings is Erridupizir.</s></p>
<p t="219380" d="4019" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">In his inscriptions in Nippur,
the holiest city of Mesopotamia,
he introduces himself like this:</s></p>
<p t="223440" d="660" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">"King</s></p>
<p t="224140" d="3220" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="2">of Gutium, king of the
four corners of the world."</s></p>
<p t="227400" d="3619" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">This is the very title used
by the Akkadian emperors.</s></p>
<p t="231060" d="3600" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">His inscriptions describe his campaigns
against rebellious regions and how</s></p>
<p t="234700" d="3740" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">he dedicated the spoils to
the temple of the god Enlil.</s></p>
<p t="238500" d="3280" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">Under the Gutians, the Sumerian
cities also enjoyed a brilliant era.</s></p>
<p t="241820" d="4379" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">Lagash, under Prince Gudea, built
temples and lived through a golden age</s></p>
<p t="246240" d="880" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">of art.</s></p>
<p t="247160" d="3299" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">So the Gutians were not raiders
who plundered and withdrew.</s></p>
<p t="250500" d="3680" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">They were rulers who inherited the
imperial legacy of Mesopotamia.</s></p>
<p t="254220" d="4400" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">Leaders chosen by council, a confederation
of tribes and local autonomy...</s></p>
<p t="258820" d="4099" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">This is exactly the picture we will
meet thousands of years later in the</s></p>
<p t="262960" d="1839" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">social structure of the Kurds.</s></p>
<p t="270220" d="2879" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">About a hundred years
later, Utu-hengal, king of</s></p>
<p t="273140" d="2499" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">Uruk, launched a great
revolt against the Gutians.</s></p>
<p t="275680" d="2379" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">In his victory inscription
he speaks of the Gutians like</s></p>
<p t="278100" d="3379" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="2">this: "Gutium, the fanged
serpent of the mountains,</s></p>
<p t="281520" d="1639" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">the dragon of the mountains."</s></p>
<p t="283380" d="2980" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">At that time the last Gutian
king, Tirigan, was on the throne.</s></p>
<p t="286400" d="4060" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">According to the Sumerian King List,
Tirigan reigned for only forty days.</s></p>
<p t="290580" d="2279" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">Utu-hengal marched north with his army.</s></p>
<p t="292900" d="3279" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">He defeated and captured the two
commanders Tirigan sent against him.</s></p>
<p t="296220" d="4119" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">At the end of the war, Tirigan abandoned
his army and fled to a city called Dubrum.</s></p>
<p t="300380" d="3840" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">But the people of Dubrum seized him
and handed him over to Utu-hengal.</s></p>
<p t="304280" d="4659" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">Soon after, Ur-Nammu founded a new dynasty
in the city of Ur, but the struggle did</s></p>
<p t="308980" d="620" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">not end.</s></p>
<p t="309640" d="3939" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">According to some later Sumerian
texts, even Ur-Nammu lost his life</s></p>
<p t="313620" d="2199" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">in a battle against the Gutians.</s></p>
<p t="315860" d="3579" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">The political rule of the Gutians
in Mesopotamia had come to an end.</s></p>
<p t="319480" d="1560" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">But the Gutians did not disappear.</s></p>
<p t="326460" d="4119" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">For the next fifteen hundred years,
the name Gutium never vanished</s></p>
<p t="330620" d="1120" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">from Mesopotamian sources.</s></p>
<p t="331780" d="3698" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">Hammurabi, king of Babylon, recorded
his victories over the army of</s></p>
<p t="335519" d="761" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">Gutium.</s></p>
<p t="336320" d="3139" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">Assyrian kings led campaigns
into the land of Qutu.</s></p>
<p t="339500" d="4479" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">When the Persian king Cyrus
the Great took Babylon in 539</s></p>
<p t="344020" d="3739" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">BC, he listed Gutium among the
lands under his rule on the</s></p>
<p t="347800" d="680" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">Cyrus Cylinder.</s></p>
<p t="348520" d="3679" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">And the first commander to enter
Babylon was Ugbaru, governor of Gutium.</s></p>
<p t="352240" d="4000" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">So for seventeen hundred years
the name Gutium went on describing</s></p>
<p t="356280" d="1860" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">the people east of Mesopotamia.</s></p>
<p t="363560" d="3179" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">The Gutians were not the only
ancient people living in these lands.</s></p>
<p t="366780" d="4480" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">The Lullubi, the Hurrians, the Kassites
and the Medes also lived to the north</s></p>
<p t="371300" d="1199" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">and east of Mesopotamia.</s></p>
<p t="372540" d="3999" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">According to many historians,
these peoples, together with
the Gutians, are the layers that</s></p>
<p t="376580" d="959" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">formed the Kurds.</s></p>
<p t="377580" d="3759" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">Foremost among those who voiced
this link most strongly are foreign</s></p>
<p t="381380" d="500" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">historians.</s></p>
<p t="381920" d="3139" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">The orientalist Arshak Safrastian,
working in England, sees the Gutians</s></p>
<p t="385100" d="4839" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">directly as the ancestors of the Kurds
in his book published in 1948, and</s></p>
<p t="389980" d="3819" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">links the name Guti to
the root of the name Kurd.</s></p>
<p t="393840" d="2439" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">The chain of names is truly striking.</s></p>
<p t="396320" d="4240" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">Assyrian records mention names such as
Qurti and Kurti for the peoples of this</s></p>
<p t="400600" d="539" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">region.</s></p>
<p t="401180" d="3819" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">The Greek commander Xenophon writes
of the warlike Kardouchoi living</s></p>
<p t="405040" d="1439" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">north of the Tigris.</s></p>
<p t="406520" d="3320" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">In Roman times the same
region was known as Corduene.</s></p>
<p t="410420" d="1839" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">G. R. Driver of Oxford</s></p>
<p t="412300" d="3740" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">University studied how the name
Kurd relates to these ancient names.</s></p>
<p t="416120" d="4199" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">Vladimir Minorsky emphasized the role of
the Medes in the formation of the Kurds.</s></p>
<p t="420420" d="4219" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">Ephraim Speiser described the Gutians
as the native people of the lands east</s></p>
<p t="424680" d="839" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">of Mesopotamia.</s></p>
<p t="425560" d="3979" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">Mehrdad Izady argues that the Gutians
and the other ancient peoples form</s></p>
<p t="429580" d="1720" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">the foundation of the Kurdish people.</s></p>
<p t="431340" d="3379" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">The Kurdish historian Muhammad
Amin Zaki Beg holds the same view.</s></p>
<p t="435120" d="2019" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">Of course, there are
opposing views as well.</s></p>
<p t="437180" d="4199" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">The main reason is that very few documents
in the Gutian language have survived.</s></p>
<p t="441420" d="2560" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">All we have are a few names of kings.</s></p>
<p t="444120" d="4699" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">Yet geography, social structure and the
continuity of names over thousands of years</s></p>
<p t="448860" d="1439" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">make this thesis strong.</s></p>
<p t="451520" d="3439" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">The plains of Mesopotamia
were rich but defenseless.</s></p>
<p t="455000" d="2800" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">Empires came, cities were burned.</s></p>
<p t="457859" d="4139" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">In the thirteenth century the
Mongols razed Baghdad and all of</s></p>
<p t="462039" d="500" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">Mesopotamia.</s></p>
<p t="462580" d="4139" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">But thanks to the strategic
advantage of the mountains, the Kurds
were not hit by this destruction</s></p>
<p t="466760" d="2060" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">as hard as the peoples of the plains.</s></p>
<p t="468900" d="1960" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">The Kurds have an ancient saying:</s></p>
<p t="471159" d="3644" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="2">Ji çiyan pê ve, hevalên me tune.</s></p>
<p t="475003" d="1960" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">We have no friends but the mountains.</s></p>
<p t="477203" d="4200" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">The Gutians were the people who brought
down Mesopotamia's first empire and</s></p>
<p t="481443" d="1079" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">inherited its legacy.</s></p>
<p t="482563" d="4120" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">One of the first pages of Kurdish history
was written right here, in these lands.</s></p>
<p t="486984" d="3779" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">If you enjoyed this video, don't
forget to subscribe and share your</s></p>
<p t="490804" d="1379" wp="1" ws="1"><s p="1">thoughts in the comments.</s></p>
</body>
</timedtext>
